--TEST--
`Core\Html::toSource` refuses an empty reason and accepts any written one — ADR 0133 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0133 § 3's second half: the hatch is rare, greppable, and carries a
// written reason at the site. An empty reason is refused, because a reason
// nobody had to write is a reason nobody wrote — the same argument `unsafe`
// carries in this project's own policy.
Core\Html\Markup $m = "<hr>" as Core\Html\Markup;

try {
    echo Core\Html::toSource($m, ""), "\n";
    echo "opened\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// Both bounds named together, so a member that refused every reason — or that
// only looked at the first byte — fails on one of these two lines while still
// printing plausibly on the other. One character is a written reason as far as
// this rule is concerned: § 3 asks that something be written, and judging
// whether it is a *good* reason is the reader's job and not the runtime's.
echo Core\Html::toSource($m, "."), "\n";

// Whitespace is written text too. The rule is emptiness and nothing subtler,
// which keeps it a rule a reader can predict rather than a lint.
echo Core\Html::toSource($m, " "), "\n";

// And the ordinary case, which is what every real call site looks like.
echo Core\Html::toSource($m, "writing the fragment to a cache that stores text"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Html::toSource(): the reason is written for the next reader, so an empty one is refused
<hr>
<hr>
<hr>
